/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 56-60
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:56, name:"A House of Prayer for All People", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 56:7",verse:"Even them will I bring to my holy mountain, and make them joyful in my house of prayer: their burnt offerings and their sacrifices shall be accepted upon mine altar; for mine house shall be called an house of prayer for all people."}, facts:["Did you know the eunuchs and foreigners of Isaiah 56 were kept at a distance by the old law, yet God gives them a place and a name better than sons and daughters? Grace reaches past the old boundaries.","Did you know Jesus quoted this chapter when He cleansed the temple? 'My house shall be called a house of prayer' is Isaiah 56:7.","Did you know a watchdog that will not bark is the Bible's picture of a failed leader? The 'dumb dogs' of verse 10 slept while danger came."], questions:[
{q:"What two things does God command His people to keep and do?", options:["Keep judgment and do justice","Keep the feasts and do penance","Keep silence and do nothing","Keep the fasts and do sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Keep ye judgment, and do justice: for my salvation is near to come, and my righteousness to be revealed.", insight:"Judgment and justice are not moods but practices. God ties His near salvation to how His people treat each other."},
{q:"What is near to come?", options:["God's salvation","The sabbath day","The promised land","The day of vengeance"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Keep ye judgment, and do justice: for my salvation is near to come, and my righteousness to be revealed.", insight:"God's salvation is described as near, not distant. Hope in this chapter always has a short horizon."},
{q:"Who is called blessed?", options:["The man who does this and lays hold on it","The priest who offers daily","The rich man who gives alms","The elder who sits at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:2",verse:"Blessed is the man that doeth this, and the son of man that layeth hold on it; that keepeth the sabbath from polluting it, and keepeth his hand from doing any evil.", insight:"Blessing falls on the doer, not the hearer. 'Layeth hold' is a gripping word for a gripping faith."},
{q:"What two habits mark the blessed man?", options:["He keeps the sabbath from polluting it, and his hand from doing any evil","He fasts twice a week and tithes all he owns","He offers a lamb each morning and evening","He prays at dawn and gives to the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:2",verse:"Blessed is the man that doeth this, and the son of man that layeth hold on it; that keepeth the sabbath from polluting it, and keepeth his hand from doing any evil.", insight:"Holiness here is a rhythm and a restraint: one day guarded for God, one hand guarded from harm."},
{q:"What must the foreigner who has joined himself to the LORD stop saying?", options:["'The LORD hath utterly separated me from his people'","'I am too sinful to be forgiven'","'The temple is only for Israel'","'My offering will be rejected'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:3",verse:"Neither let the son of the stranger, that hath joined himself to the LORD, speak, saying, The LORD hath utterly separated me from his people: neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree.", insight:"Joining the LORD ends the outsider story. God will not let His adopted children talk like strangers."},
{q:"What must the eunuch stop calling himself?", options:["A dry tree","A broken branch","A withered leaf","A barren field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:3",verse:"Neither let the son of the stranger, that hath joined himself to the LORD, speak, saying, The LORD hath utterly separated me from his people: neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree.", insight:"God renames what shame named. Fruitfulness in His house is not measured by children."},
{q:"Which three things mark the eunuchs God accepts?", options:["They keep His sabbaths, choose what pleases Him, and take hold of His covenant","They serve in the temple, sing in the choir, and keep the feasts","They pray daily, fast weekly, and give generously","They study the law, teach the children, and guard the gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:4",verse:"For thus saith the LORD unto the eunuchs that keep my sabbaths, and choose the things that please me, and take hold of my covenant;", insight:"Acceptance rests on covenant loyalty, not on the body or the bloodline."},
{q:"To whom does the LORD speak this promise?", options:["Unto the eunuchs","Unto the priests","Unto the elders","Unto the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:4",verse:"For thus saith the LORD unto the eunuchs that keep my sabbaths, and choose the things that please me, and take hold of my covenant;", insight:"God addresses the excluded directly. The promise has their name on it."},
{q:"What does God promise them within His house and walls?", options:["A place and a name better than sons and daughters","Riches and lands beyond the Jordan","Long life and many descendants","Seats of honor at the feasts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:5",verse:"Even unto them will I give in mine house and within my walls a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.", insight:"God compensates what life withheld, and His compensation is better than what was lost."},
{q:"What kind of name will He give them?", options:["An everlasting name that shall not be cut off","A famous name among the nations","A new name written in the law","A royal name among princes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:5",verse:"Even unto them will I give in mine house and within my walls a place and a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off.", insight:"An everlasting name means you are remembered where it matters: in God's house, forever."},
{q:"The strangers who join the LORD come to do what three things?", options:["Serve Him, love His name, and be His servants","Build the walls, guard the gates, and keep the stores","Learn the law, teach their children, and pay the tax","Sing the psalms, play instruments, and keep silence"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:6",verse:"Also the sons of the stranger, that join themselves to the LORD, to serve him, and to love the name of the LORD, to be his servants, every one that keepeth the sabbath from polluting it, and taketh hold of my covenant;", insight:"Service, love, and belonging. That is the whole job description of the outsider brought in."},
{q:"What must they keep from polluting?", options:["The sabbath","The temple courts","The altar","The priesthood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:6",verse:"Also the sons of the stranger, that join themselves to the LORD, to serve him, and to love the name of the LORD, to be his servants, every one that keepeth the sabbath from polluting it, and taketh hold of my covenant;", insight:"The sabbath was God's sign of covenant rest. Keeping it unpolluted was how the stranger showed whose he was."},
{q:"What do the drunken leaders say they will fetch?", options:["Wine","Water for washing","Oil for the lamps","Bread for the feast"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:12",verse:"Come ye, say they, I will fetch wine, and we will fill ourselves with strong drink; and to morrow shall be as this day, and much more abundant.", insight:"Their answer to everything is another drink. Escapism had become their liturgy."},
{q:"What do they promise about tomorrow?", options:["It shall be as this day, and much more abundant","It will bring judgment on the wicked","It will be a day of fasting","It will be better for the poor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:12",verse:"Come ye, say they, I will fetch wine, and we will fill ourselves with strong drink; and to morrow shall be as this day, and much more abundant.", insight:"The drunkard's gospel is more of the same, only more. God offers a tomorrow that is actually new."},
{q:"Besides the outcasts of Israel, whom else will God gather?", options:["Others besides those already gathered","Only the priests and Levites","The kings of the earth","The armies of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:8",verse:"The Lord GOD which gathereth the outcasts of Israel saith, Yet will I gather others to him, beside those that are gathered unto him.", insight:"God's gathering is always larger than ours. There is room for others besides."},
{q:"Who is summoned to come and devour?", options:["All the beasts of the field and of the forest","The lions of the wilderness","The birds of the air","The armies of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:9",verse:"All ye beasts of the field, come to devour, yea, all ye beasts in the forest.", insight:"Failed shepherds invite hungry wolves. Neglect has consequences the prophet will not soften."},
{q:"What are the blind, ignorant watchmen compared to?", options:["Dumb dogs that cannot bark","Deaf shepherds with no staff","Blind guides in a ditch","Sleeping guards at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:10",verse:"His watchmen are blind: they are all ignorant, they are all dumb dogs, they cannot bark; sleeping, lying down, loving to slumber.", insight:"A watchdog that will not bark is worse than no watchdog. Silence in danger is a betrayal."},
{q:"What three drowsy habits do the watchmen love?", options:["Sleeping, lying down, and loving to slumber","Eating, drinking, and making merry","Talking, laughing, and telling stories","Wandering, dreaming, and forgetting"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:10",verse:"His watchmen are blind: they are all ignorant, they are all dumb dogs, they cannot bark; sleeping, lying down, loving to slumber.", insight:"Comfort can be a calling killer. The watchman's bed is the flock's danger."},
{q:"What kind of dogs are the greedy leaders?", options:["Greedy dogs which can never have enough","Wild dogs that tear the sheep","Lazy dogs that sleep all day","Stray dogs with no master"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:11",verse:"Yea, they are greedy dogs which can never have enough, and they are shepherds that cannot understand: they all look to their own way, every one for his gain, from his quarter.", insight:"Greed is a hunger that eating never fixes. 'Never enough' is its honest confession."},
{q:"What do these shepherds who cannot understand look to?", options:["Their own way, every one for his gain","The stars for guidance","The king for orders","The temple for comfort"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 56:11",verse:"Yea, they are greedy dogs which can never have enough, and they are shepherds that cannot understand: they all look to their own way, every one for his gain, from his quarter.", insight:"A shepherd with his eyes on his own gain has stopped watching the sheep."}
]},
{n:57, name:"No Peace to the Wicked", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 57:15",verse:"For thus saith the high and lofty One that inhabiteth eternity, whose name is Holy; I dwell in the high and holy place, with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble, and to revive the heart of the contrite ones."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah says the righteous are taken away 'from the evil to come'? For the godly, death can be a rescue, not a tragedy.","Did you know Paul quotes Isaiah 57:19 in Ephesians 2? 'Peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near' becomes the gospel for Gentiles brought near.","Did you know 'There is no peace, saith my God, to the wicked' is Isaiah's refrain? He sounds it here and back in 48:22."], questions:[
{q:"When the righteous perishes, what does no man do?", options:["Lay it to heart","Weep at the grave","Sing a lament","Mark the day"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:1",verse:"The righteous perisheth, and no man layeth it to heart: and merciful men are taken away, none considering that the righteous is taken away from the evil to come.", insight:"The world scrolls past the death of the godly. Heaven does not."},
{q:"The one who walks in uprightness shall rest where?", options:["In his bed","In a borrowed tomb","On the holy mountain","Under the altar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:2",verse:"He shall enter into peace: they shall rest in their beds, each one walking in his uprightness.", insight:"'He shall enter into peace' is God's obituary for the upright: rest, not ruin."},
{q:"Who is told to draw near for judgment?", options:["The sons of the sorceress, the seed of the adulterer and the whore","The elders of the city and the priests","The merchants and the money changers","The kings and their counselors"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:3",verse:"But draw near hither, ye sons of the sorceress, the seed of the adulterer and the whore.", insight:"God names the parentage of sin plainly. Flattery never helped anyone repent."},
{q:"What mocking gestures did they make?", options:["A wide mouth and a drawn-out tongue","A clenched fist and a stamped foot","A turned back and a closed ear","A shaking head and folded arms"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:4",verse:"Against whom do ye sport yourselves? against whom make ye a wide mouth, and draw out the tongue? are ye not children of transgression, a seed of falsehood,", insight:"Mockery is worship in reverse. The mouth made to praise God was used to jeer Him."},
{q:"Under what did they inflame themselves with idols?", options:["Every green tree","Every high tower","Every carved image","Every city gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:5",verse:"Enflaming yourselves with idols under every green tree, slaying the children in the valleys under the clifts of the rocks?", insight:"Idolatry loved the shade of green trees. Sin always wants a pretty setting."},
{q:"Where is the idolater's portion and lot?", options:["Among the smooth stones of the stream","In the palaces of the king","Under the altar of brass","In the treasuries of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:6",verse:"Among the smooth stones of the stream is thy portion; they, they are thy lot: even to them hast thou poured a drink offering, thou hast offered a meat offering. Should I receive comfort in these?", insight:"The smooth stones they worshiped became their inheritance. We become like what we adore."},
{q:"Where did she set her bed and go up to offer sacrifice?", options:["Upon a lofty and high mountain","In the valley of Hinnom","Behind the temple veil","On the roof of her house"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:7",verse:"Upon a lofty and high mountain hast thou set thy bed: even thither wentest thou up to offer sacrifice.", insight:"She climbed high to worship low things. Elevation never sanctifies idolatry."},
{q:"To whom did she go with ointment and increased perfumes?", options:["The king","The high priest","The prophet","The merchant"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:9",verse:"And thou wentest to the king with ointment, and didst increase thy perfumes, and didst send thy messengers far off, and didst debase thyself even unto hell.", insight:"She courted earthly power with perfume instead of seeking God with prayer. Alliances replaced allegiance."},
{q:"Though wearied in the greatness of her way, what did she refuse to say?", options:["'There is no hope'","'I am too tired'","'The way is too long'","'God has forgotten me'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:10",verse:"Thou art wearied in the greatness of thy way; yet saidst thou not, There is no hope: thou hast found the life of thine hand; therefore thou wast not grieved.", insight:"False religion never admits despair. It keeps striving because stopping would mean facing the truth."},
{q:"God asks: you were afraid of whom, that you did what?", options:["Lied, and did not remember Him","Fled, and hid in the caves","Stole, and bribed the judges","Fought, and trusted in chariots"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:11",verse:"And of whom hast thou been afraid or feared, that thou hast lied, and hast not remembered me, nor laid it to thy heart? have not I held my peace even of old, and thou fearest me not?", insight:"Fear of people makes liars of us. Fear of God makes truth tellers."},
{q:"What will God declare, and what good will it do them?", options:["Their righteousness and works, which shall not profit them","Their sins and sorrows, which shall be forgiven","Their prayers and fasts, which shall be heard","Their gifts and vows, which shall be accepted"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:12",verse:"I will declare thy righteousness, and thy works; for they shall not profit thee.", insight:"Self-made righteousness is declared only to be dismissed. It cannot profit where it counts."},
{q:"When she cries out, what will become of her idol companies?", options:["The wind shall carry them all away","The fire shall consume them","The earth shall swallow them","The sea shall cover them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:13",verse:"When thou criest, let thy companies deliver thee; but the wind shall carry them all away; vanity shall take them: but he that putteth his trust in me shall possess the land, and shall inherit my holy mountain;", insight:"Idols cannot carry you in trouble. They are carried away like chaff."},
{q:"Who shall possess the land and inherit the holy mountain?", options:["He that putteth his trust in me","He that offereth the most sacrifices","He that keepeth every feast day","He that buildeth the highest altar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:13",verse:"When thou criest, let thy companies deliver thee; but the wind shall carry them all away; vanity shall take them: but he that putteth his trust in me shall possess the land, and shall inherit my holy mountain;", insight:"The whole inheritance hangs on one verb: trust."},
{q:"What must be taken up out of the way of God's people?", options:["The stumblingblock","The heavy taxes","The foreign armies","The ruined walls"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:14",verse:"And shall say, Cast ye up, cast ye up, prepare the way, take up the stumblingblock out of the way of my people.", insight:"God clears the road for returning sinners. Grace removes what would trip the penitent."},
{q:"Why will God not contend forever nor be always wroth?", options:["The spirit would fail before Him, and the souls He made","The nations would mock His people","The covenant would be broken","The angels would grow weary"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:16",verse:"For I will not contend for ever, neither will I be always wroth: for the spirit should fail before me, and the souls which I have made.", insight:"God tempers His anger with mercy because He remembers we are His making."},
{q:"For what iniquity was God wroth, and smote him?", options:["His covetousness","His idolatry","His Sabbath breaking","His murder"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:17",verse:"For the iniquity of his covetousness was I wroth, and smote him: I hid me, and was wroth, and he went on frowardly in the way of his heart.", insight:"Covetousness drew God's anger because it is idolatry with a wallet: wanting more than God."},
{q:"'I have seen his ways,' says God, 'and I will' do what?", options:["Heal him","Judge him","Forsake him","Forget him"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:18",verse:"I have seen his ways, and will heal him: I will lead him also, and restore comforts unto him and to his mourners.", insight:"God sees the worst ways and answers with healing. Being fully known did not disqualify him."},
{q:"What does God create?", options:["The fruit of the lips","The songs of the morning","The prayers of the saints","The praises of angels"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:19",verse:"I create the fruit of the lips; Peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near, saith the LORD; and I will heal him.", insight:"Peace on the lips is God's creation, not our achievement. He grows the fruit; we bear it."},
{q:"To whom does God speak peace?", options:["To him that is far off, and to him that is near","To the priests and the Levites only","To the righteous of Israel alone","To the kings of the earth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:19",verse:"I create the fruit of the lips; Peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near, saith the LORD; and I will heal him.", insight:"Far off and near are both included. No one is too distant for this peace to reach."},
{q:"The wicked are like what?", options:["The troubled sea, whose waters cast up mire and dirt","The withered grass that the wind drives","The broken cistern that holds no water","The barren fig tree by the wayside"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 57:20",verse:"But the wicked are like the troubled sea, when it cannot rest, whose waters cast up mire and dirt.", insight:"Wickedness cannot rest because it has no bottom. The churned sea keeps throwing up its own filth."}
]},
{n:58, name:"The Fast God Chooses", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 58:7",verse:"Is it not to deal thy bread to the hungry, and that thou bring the poor that are cast out to thy house? when thou seest the naked, that thou cover him; and that thou hide not thyself from thine own flesh?"}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 58 redefines fasting? God's chosen fast looses chains, frees the oppressed, and feeds the hungry; it never was about looking miserable.","Did you know 'thou shalt be like a watered garden' was promised to exiles? God's refreshment does not wait for better circumstances.","Did you know 'the repairer of the breach' is one of Scripture's great job titles? It belongs to anyone who rebuilds what sin tore down."], questions:[
{q:"Like what must the prophet lift up his voice?", options:["Like a trumpet","Like a thunderclap","Like a rushing wind","Like a lion's roar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:1",verse:"Cry aloud, spare not, lift up thy voice like a trumpet, and shew my people their transgression, and the house of Jacob their sins.", insight:"A trumpet is not for lullabies. Some truths must be sounded loud enough to wake the sleepy."},
{q:"Whose sins must be shown to them?", options:["The house of Jacob","The nations round about","The priests alone","The kings of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:1",verse:"Cry aloud, spare not, lift up thy voice like a trumpet, and shew my people their transgression, and the house of Jacob their sins.", insight:"God starts the rebuke at home. His own people get the first trumpet blast."},
{q:"Though far from God, what did the people still do daily?", options:["Seek Him daily and delight to know His ways","Offer the morning sacrifice","Gather at the temple gates","Sing the psalms of ascent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:2",verse:"Yet they seek me daily, and delight to know my ways, as a nation that did righteousness, and forsook not the ordinance of their God: they ask of me the ordinances of justice; they take delight in approaching to God.", insight:"Religious routine can survive a dead heart. They sought God daily and missed Him entirely."},
{q:"What did they ask of God?", options:["The ordinances of justice","Gold and silver","Victory in battle","Rain in season"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:2",verse:"Yet they seek me daily, and delight to know my ways, as a nation that did righteousness, and forsook not the ordinance of their God: they ask of me the ordinances of justice; they take delight in approaching to God.", insight:"They wanted God's justice for others while practicing injustice themselves."},
{q:"What was the fasting people's complaint?", options:["'We fasted, and thou seest not'","'We prayed, and thou heardest not'","'We gave, and thou blessedst not'","'We wept, and thou comfortedst not'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:3",verse:"Wherefore have we fasted, say they, and thou seest not? wherefore have we afflicted our soul, and thou takest no knowledge? Behold, in the day of your fast ye find pleasure, and exact all your labours.", insight:"They treated fasting like a transaction and were angry the receipt never came."},
{q:"What did they do on the day of their fast?", options:["Found pleasure and exacted all their labours","Feasted with their neighbors","Slept through the daylight hours","Journeyed to the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:3",verse:"Wherefore have we fasted, say they, and thou seest not? wherefore have we afflicted our soul, and thou takest no knowledge? Behold, in the day of your fast ye find pleasure, and exact all your labours.", insight:"A fast that changes nothing about how you treat people is just a skipped meal."},
{q:"What did their fasting produce?", options:["Strife and debate, and smiting with the fist of wickedness","Humility and quiet prayer","Generosity to the poor","Peace in their homes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:4",verse:"Behold, ye fast for strife and debate, and to smite with the fist of wickedness: ye shall not fast as ye do this day, to make your voice to be heard on high.", insight:"Fasting that ends in fistfights was never worship. It was hunger with a halo."},
{q:"What picture of humiliation does God reject?", options:["Bowing the head as a bulrush","Tearing one's garments","Weeping between the porch and the altar","Lying prostrate all night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:5",verse:"Is it such a fast that I have chosen? a day for a man to afflict his soul? is it to bow down his head as a bulrush, and to spread sackcloth and ashes under him? wilt thou call this a fast, and an acceptable day to the LORD?", insight:"God is not impressed by posture. A bowed head over an unbowed heart fools no one."},
{q:"What did they spread under themselves?", options:["Sackcloth and ashes","Palm branches and reeds","Fine linen and purple","Straw and goatskins"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:5",verse:"Is it such a fast that I have chosen? a day for a man to afflict his soul? is it to bow down his head as a bulrush, and to spread sackcloth and ashes under him? wilt thou call this a fast, and an acceptable day to the LORD?", insight:"Sackcloth without surrender is costume, not contrition."},
{q:"What is the first work of the fast God chooses?", options:["To loose the bands of wickedness","To bow the head like a reed","To weep for one's sins","To abstain from all food"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:6",verse:"Is not this the fast that I have chosen? to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke?", insight:"God's fast begins with somebody else's chains. True devotion always leaks out in justice."},
{q:"What must be broken?", options:["Every yoke","Every idol","Every altar","Every weapon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:6",verse:"Is not this the fast that I have chosen? to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke?", insight:"Not some yokes, every yoke. Partial liberation is still oppression for those left bound."},
{q:"What shall break forth as the morning?", options:["Thy light","Thy glory","Thy harvest","Thy song"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:8",verse:"Then shall thy light break forth as the morning, and thine health shall spring forth speedily: and thy righteousness shall go before thee; the glory of the LORD shall be thy rereward.", insight:"Light comes to the merciful like sunrise: surely, and all at once."},
{q:"What shall be thy rereward?", options:["The glory of the LORD","The armies of Israel","The walls of the city","The shield of faith"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:8",verse:"Then shall thy light break forth as the morning, and thine health shall spring forth speedily: and thy righteousness shall go before thee; the glory of the LORD shall be thy rereward.", insight:"God Himself guards the back of the generous. The rereward is the safest place in the march."},
{q:"When thou criest, what will the LORD say?", options:["'Here I am'","'Be still and wait'","'Go in peace'","'Fear not'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:9",verse:"Then shalt thou call, and the LORD shall answer; thou shalt cry, and he shall say, Here I am. If thou take away from the midst of thee the yoke, the putting forth of the finger, and speaking vanity;", insight:"Two words, and heaven is present. The obedient never cry into an empty room."},
{q:"If we satisfy the afflicted soul, what becomes of our darkness?", options:["It shall be as the noonday","It shall flee like shadows","It shall turn to starlight","It shall hide in caves"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:10",verse:"And if thou draw out thy soul to the hungry, and satisfy the afflicted soul; then shall thy light rise in obscurity, and thy darkness be as the noonday:", insight:"Feeding the hungry is how God brightens the giver's own night."},
{q:"In drought the LORD will satisfy thy soul and do what to thy bones?", options:["Make fat thy bones","Strengthen them like iron","Clothe them with flesh","Hide them in the rock"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:11",verse:"And the LORD shall guide thee continually, and satisfy thy soul in drought, and make fat thy bones: and thou shalt be like a watered garden, and like a spring of water, whose waters fail not.", insight:"God's care reaches the marrow. Drought cannot starve the one He feeds."},
{q:"What two titles will the rebuilders carry?", options:["'The repairer of the breach' and 'The restorer of paths to dwell in'","'The keeper of the law' and 'The teacher of Israel'","'The watchman on the wall' and 'The trumpet of Zion'","'The servant of the LORD' and 'The friend of God'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:12",verse:"And they that shall be of thee shall build the old waste places: thou shalt raise up the foundations of many generations; and thou shalt be called, The repairer of the breach, The restorer of paths to dwell in.", insight:"Rebuilding what sin broke is a holy calling with a holy name."},
{q:"What shall the Sabbath be called?", options:["A delight, the holy of the LORD, honourable","A burden, the yoke of the LORD, heavy","A custom, the way of the fathers, old","A rest, the gift of the week, quiet"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:13",verse:"If thou turn away thy foot from the sabbath, from doing thy pleasure on my holy day; and call the sabbath a delight, the holy of the LORD, honourable; and shalt honour him, not doing thine own ways, nor finding thine own pleasure, nor speaking thine own words:", insight:"Call it a delight and it becomes one. The Sabbath is a feast day, not a fast day."},
{q:"The faithful shall be caused to ride where?", options:["Upon the high places of the earth","Upon horses of war","Upon the clouds of heaven","Upon the walls of Zion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:14",verse:"Then shalt thou delight thyself in the LORD; and I will cause thee to ride upon the high places of the earth, and feed thee with the heritage of Jacob thy father: for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"Obedience lifts. The lowly path of Sabbath keeping ends on the high places."},
{q:"With what will God feed them?", options:["The heritage of Jacob thy father","The finest of the wheat","The fruit of the land","Manna from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 58:14",verse:"Then shalt thou delight thyself in the LORD; and I will cause thee to ride upon the high places of the earth, and feed thee with the heritage of Jacob thy father: for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"God feeds His faithful from the family inheritance. The promises made to Jacob are still on the table."}
]},
{n:59, name:"The Hand Is Not Shortened", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 59:21",verse:"As for me, this is my covenant with them, saith the LORD; My spirit that is upon thee, and my words which I have put in thy mouth, shall not depart out of thy mouth, nor out of the mouth of thy seed, nor out of the mouth of thy seed’s seed, saith the LORD, from henceforth and for ever."}, facts:["Did you know Paul quotes Isaiah 59:7-8 in Romans 3? 'Their feet are swift to shed blood' becomes part of his case that all have sinned.","Did you know the armor of Isaiah 59:17 is the Old Testament wardrobe behind Ephesians 6? The breastplate of righteousness and helmet of salvation start here.","Did you know this chapter opens with the problem and closes with the promise? 'The LORD's hand is not shortened' leads at last to 'the Redeemer shall come to Zion.'"], questions:[
{q:"What two things about God have not failed?", options:["His hand is not shortened and His ear is not heavy","His arm is not weary and His eye is not dim","His word is not broken and His oath is not void","His mercy is not spent and His love is not cold"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:1",verse:"Behold, the LORD’S hand is not shortened, that it cannot save; neither his ear heavy, that it cannot hear:", insight:"The problem was never God's reach or God's hearing. Both were fine."},
{q:"What has separated between you and your God?", options:["Your iniquities","Your enemies","Your priests","Your kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:2",verse:"But your iniquities have separated between you and your God, and your sins have hid his face from you, that he will not hear.", insight:"Sin is a wall builder. It separates before it destroys."},
{q:"What has hid His face, so He will not hear?", options:["Your sins","Your sorrows","Your sacrifices","Your songs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:2",verse:"But your iniquities have separated between you and your God, and your sins have hid his face from you, that he will not hear.", insight:"Unanswered prayer is not always unheard prayer. Sometimes sin is covering God's face."},
{q:"With what are their hands defiled and their fingers stained?", options:["Blood and iniquity","Bribes and extortion","Idols and carvings","Wine and strong drink"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:3",verse:"For your hands are defiled with blood, and your fingers with iniquity; your lips have spoken lies, your tongue hath muttered perverseness.", insight:"Hands tell the truth about hearts. What we do stains deeper than what we say."},
{q:"None calls for justice or pleads for truth; instead they do what?", options:["Trust in vanity, speak lies, conceive mischief, and bring forth iniquity","Hide in caves and flee to the hills","Bribe the judges and silence the witnesses","Worship idols and burn incense"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:4",verse:"None calleth for justice, nor any pleadeth for truth: they trust in vanity, and speak lies; they conceive mischief, and bring forth iniquity.", insight:"A society that stops pleading for truth starts producing lies like a harvest."},
{q:"What eggs do they hatch?", options:["Cockatrice' eggs","Serpent's eggs","Adder's eggs","Dragon's eggs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:5",verse:"They hatch cockatrice’ eggs, and weave the spider’s web: he that eateth of their eggs dieth, and that which is crushed breaketh out into a viper.", insight:"Evil has a hatchery. What looks harmless in the nest is deadly when grown."},
{q:"What breaks out of the crushed egg?", options:["A viper","A scorpion","A wasp","A spider"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:5",verse:"They hatch cockatrice’ eggs, and weave the spider’s web: he that eateth of their eggs dieth, and that which is crushed breaketh out into a viper.", insight:"Crush sin early or it hatches. Half measures with evil only release it."},
{q:"Where do their feet run, and in what haste?", options:["To evil, hasting to shed innocent blood","To the temple, hasting to offer sacrifice","To the city gates, hasting to trade","To the hills, hasting to hide"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:7",verse:"Their feet run to evil, and they make haste to shed innocent blood: their thoughts are thoughts of iniquity; wasting and destruction are in their paths.", insight:"Feet reveal direction. Some people do not drift into evil; they run to it."},
{q:"What way do they not know?", options:["The way of peace","The way of the sea","The way of the stars","The way of the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:8",verse:"The way of peace they know not; and there is no judgment in their goings: they have made them crooked paths: whosoever goeth therein shall not know peace.", insight:"You can be an expert in crooked paths and a stranger to peace."},
{q:"They wait for light, but what do they find?", options:["Obscurity; they walk in darkness","A dim and distant dawn","The shadow of death","A borrowed lamp"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:9",verse:"Therefore is judgment far from us, neither doth justice overtake us: we wait for light, but behold obscurity; for brightness, but we walk in darkness.", insight:"Waiting for light while walking away from God is waiting for sunrise at midnight, facing west."},
{q:"Like whom do they grope for the wall?", options:["The blind, as if they had no eyes","Children in a dark room","Travelers in a fog","Miners in a pit"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:10",verse:"We grope for the wall like the blind, and we grope as if we had no eyes: we stumble at noonday as in the night; we are in desolate places as dead men.", insight:"Sin blinds at noon. They stumble in daylight as if it were night."},
{q:"Like what do they roar and mourn?", options:["Bears and doves","Lions and lambs","Wolves and owls","Bulls and sparrows"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:11",verse:"We roar all like bears, and mourn sore like doves: we look for judgment, but there is none; for salvation, but it is far off from us.", insight:"Their sounds are animal, not human: roaring like bears, moaning like doves, with no words left for prayer."},
{q:"What testifies against them?", options:["Their sins","Their neighbors","Their children","The prophets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:12",verse:"For our transgressions are multiplied before thee, and our sins testify against us: for our transgressions are with us; and as for our iniquities, we know them;", insight:"Sin is its own witness. It testifies in court against the sinner."},
{q:"What did they utter from the heart?", options:["Words of falsehood","Curses against God","Oaths by the temple","Lies about the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:13",verse:"In transgressing and lying against the LORD, and departing away from our God, speaking oppression and revolt, conceiving and uttering from the heart words of falsehood.", insight:"The heart is a spring. What it conceives, the mouth eventually utters."},
{q:"What lies fallen in the street while equity cannot enter?", options:["Truth","Justice","Mercy","Wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:14",verse:"And judgment is turned away backward, and justice standeth afar off: for truth is fallen in the street, and equity cannot enter.", insight:"When truth falls in the street, the whole city trips over it."},
{q:"What displeased the LORD?", options:["That there was no judgment","That the temple was empty","That the priests were silent","That the land was barren"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:15",verse:"Yea, truth faileth; and he that departeth from evil maketh himself a prey: and the LORD saw it, and it displeased him that there was no judgment.", insight:"God is displeased not only by evil done but by justice left undone."},
{q:"At what did God wonder?", options:["That there was no intercessor","That the people still sacrificed","That the prophets still spoke","That the city still stood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:16",verse:"And he saw that there was no man, and wondered that there was no intercessor: therefore his arm brought salvation unto him; and his righteousness, it sustained him.", insight:"Heaven wondered at the silence. When no one prays for the fallen, God notices the gap."},
{q:"What armor did the LORD put on?", options:["Righteousness as a breastplate and a helmet of salvation","Justice as a sword and truth as a shield","Wrath as a spear and vengeance as a bow","Mercy as a cloak and peace as sandals"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:17",verse:"For he put on righteousness as a breastplate, and an helmet of salvation upon his head; and he put on the garments of vengeance for clothing, and was clad with zeal as a cloke.", insight:"When no intercessor stood up, God suited up. Salvation would come by His own arm."},
{q:"When the enemy comes in like a flood, what will the Spirit of the LORD do?", options:["Lift up a standard against him","Part the waters before him","Raise a wall around the city","Send fire from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:19",verse:"So shall they fear the name of the LORD from the west, and his glory from the rising of the sun. When the enemy shall come in like a flood, the Spirit of the LORD shall lift up a standard against him.", insight:"Floods are met with standards. God's Spirit rallies His people when evil surges."},
{q:"To whom shall the Redeemer come?", options:["To Zion, and to them that turn from transgression in Jacob","To the temple, and to the priests who serve","To the battlefield, and to the armies","To the palace, and to the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 59:20",verse:"And the Redeemer shall come to Zion, and unto them that turn from transgression in Jacob, saith the LORD.", insight:"The Redeemer comes to Zion, but His welcome party is the repentant."}
]},
{n:60, name:"Arise, Shine", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 60:19",verse:"The sun shall be no more thy light by day; neither for brightness shall the moon give light unto thee: but the LORD shall be unto thee an everlasting light, and thy God thy glory."}, facts:["Did you know the gold and incense of Isaiah 60:6 appear at Jesus' birth? The wise men carried those very gifts to the newborn King.","Did you know Revelation borrows Isaiah 60's ending? Gates never shut, and no need of sun or moon, return in John's New Jerusalem.","Did you know 'a little one shall become a thousand' is a favorite promise for small beginnings? God loves growing great things from tiny starts."], questions:[
{q:"What two commands open the chapter?", options:["Arise, shine","Rise, build","Awake, sing","Come, see"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:1",verse:"Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon thee.", insight:"The command comes before the light is fully seen. Faith rises first, then shines."},
{q:"Why should she shine?", options:["Her light is come, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon her","Her enemies are fled and her walls are built","Her children are grown and her barns are full","Her king is crowned and her priests are ready"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:1",verse:"Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon thee.", insight:"We shine by reflected glory. The light is His; the shining is ours."},
{q:"What covers the earth and the people?", options:["Darkness, and gross darkness","A thick cloud and heavy rain","Smoke and the dust of war","Night and the silence of death"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:2",verse:"For, behold, the darkness shall cover the earth, and gross darkness the people: but the LORD shall arise upon thee, and his glory shall be seen upon thee.", insight:"The darker the world, the brighter the contrast. Gross darkness makes glory unmistakable."},
{q:"Who shall come to thy light?", options:["The Gentiles, and kings to the brightness of thy rising","The exiles returning from Babylon","The priests carrying the ark","The shepherds with their flocks"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:3",verse:"And the Gentiles shall come to thy light, and kings to the brightness of thy rising.", insight:"God's light was never meant for one nation. Gentiles and kings are drawn to the same dawn."},
{q:"Thy sons shall come from far, and thy daughters shall be what?", options:["Nursed at thy side","Carried on shoulders","Led by the hand","Welcomed with songs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:4",verse:"Lift up thine eyes round about, and see: all they gather themselves together, they come to thee: thy sons shall come from far, and thy daughters shall be nursed at thy side.", insight:"The homecoming includes the tender and the far. No one God gathers is an afterthought."},
{q:"What shall be converted unto thee?", options:["The abundance of the sea","The harvest of the fields","The flocks of the hills","The treasures of kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:5",verse:"Then thou shalt see, and flow together, and thine heart shall fear, and be enlarged; because the abundance of the sea shall be converted unto thee, the forces of the Gentiles shall come unto thee.", insight:"Even the sea's abundance turns toward Zion. Commerce itself gets converted."},
{q:"From where do they come bringing gold and incense?", options:["Sheba","Tarshish","Egypt","Midian"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:6",verse:"The multitude of camels shall cover thee, the dromedaries of Midian and Ephah; all they from Sheba shall come: they shall bring gold and incense; and they shall shew forth the praises of the LORD.", insight:"Sheba's gold and incense foreshadow the wise men's gifts. The nations bring their best to God's King."},
{q:"What covers thee in the prophet's picture?", options:["The multitude of camels, the dromedaries of Midian and Ephah","The chariots of Egypt and Assyria","The ships of Tarshish and the isles","The armies of the east and west"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:6",verse:"The multitude of camels shall cover thee, the dromedaries of Midian and Ephah; all they from Sheba shall come: they shall bring gold and incense; and they shall shew forth the praises of the LORD.", insight:"The caravan of the nations becomes a worship procession. Trade routes turn into pilgrimage roads."},
{q:"Whose flocks shall be gathered unto thee?", options:["The flocks of Kedar","The flocks of Jacob","The flocks of the temple","The flocks of the hills"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:7",verse:"All the flocks of Kedar shall be gathered together unto thee, the rams of Nebaioth shall minister unto thee: they shall come up with acceptance on mine altar, and I will glorify the house of my glory.", insight:"Kedar's flocks on God's altar: the outsider's offering is accepted, not merely tolerated."},
{q:"To what are the comers compared?", options:["A cloud, and doves to their windows","An army with banners","A river in flood","A flock of sheep"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:8",verse:"Who are these that fly as a cloud, and as the doves to their windows?", insight:"They come like weather and like homing doves: many, swift, and sure of their destination."},
{q:"Which ships come first?", options:["The ships of Tarshish","The ships of Tyre","The ships of Egypt","The ships of Joppa"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:9",verse:"Surely the isles shall wait for me, and the ships of Tarshish first, to bring thy sons from far, their silver and their gold with them, unto the name of the LORD thy God, and to the Holy One of Israel, because he hath glorified thee.", insight:"Tarshish, the farthest port, leads the fleet. Distance is no barrier to God's gathering."},
{q:"Who shall build up thy walls?", options:["The sons of strangers","The exiles returned","The Levites appointed","The king's builders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:10",verse:"And the sons of strangers shall build up thy walls, and their kings shall minister unto thee: for in my wrath I smote thee, but in my favour have I had mercy on thee.", insight:"Former outsiders become builders. Grace turns strangers into stonemasons."},
{q:"How long shall thy gates stand open?", options:["Continually, day and night","On the feast days only","From dawn until dusk","When the king commands"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:11",verse:"Therefore thy gates shall be open continually; they shall not be shut day nor night; that men may bring unto thee the forces of the Gentiles, and that their kings may be brought.", insight:"Open gates day and night mean total security and total welcome. Fear locks doors; grace opens them."},
{q:"What becomes of the nation that will not serve thee?", options:["It shall perish, utterly wasted","It shall pay tribute yearly","It shall be exiled far away","It shall serve as slaves"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:12",verse:"For the nation and kingdom that will not serve thee shall perish; yea, those nations shall be utterly wasted.", insight:"Refusing God's city is refusing life. The warning is as plain as the promise."},
{q:"What three trees beautify the place of His sanctuary?", options:["The fir tree, the pine tree, and the box","The cedar, the oak, and the olive","The palm, the fig, and the vine","The cypress, the myrtle, and the almond"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:13",verse:"The glory of Lebanon shall come unto thee, the fir tree, the pine tree, and the box together, to beautify the place of my sanctuary; and I will make the place of my feet glorious.", insight:"Lebanon's glory becomes the sanctuary's beauty. Creation itself furnishes God's house."},
{q:"What two names shall they call thee?", options:["'The city of the LORD' and 'The Zion of the Holy One of Israel'","'The holy city' and 'The beloved of God'","'The new Jerusalem' and 'The bride adorned'","'The mountain of God' and 'The dwelling of peace'"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:14",verse:"The sons also of them that afflicted thee shall come bending unto thee; and all they that despised thee shall bow themselves down at the soles of thy feet; and they shall call thee, The city of the LORD, The Zion of the Holy One of Israel.", insight:"Her new names are all about Him. Redeemed identity is borrowed glory."},
{q:"Forsaken and hated, she becomes what?", options:["An eternal excellency, a joy of many generations","A fortress strong against all foes","A market for all nations","A garden blooming forever"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:15",verse:"Whereas thou hast been forsaken and hated, so that no man went through thee, I will make thee an eternal excellency, a joy of many generations.", insight:"God's makeovers are total: from 'no man went through thee' to 'joy of many generations.'"},
{q:"What shall she know at last?", options:["That 'I the LORD am thy Saviour and thy Redeemer, the mighty One of Jacob'","That her walls are strong and her gates are shut","That her enemies are fled and her land is healed","That her children are many and her years are long"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:16",verse:"Thou shalt also suck the milk of the Gentiles, and shalt suck the breast of kings: and thou shalt know that I the LORD am thy Saviour and thy Redeemer, the mighty One of Jacob.", insight:"All the blessings aim at one knowledge: knowing the LORD as Saviour and Redeemer."},
{q:"What two endings are promised?", options:["Her sun shall no more go down, and the days of her mourning shall be ended","Her wars shall cease, and her exiles return","Her debts shall be paid, and her fields restored","Her king shall reign, and her priests serve"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:20",verse:"Thy sun shall no more go down; neither shall thy moon withdraw itself: for the LORD shall be thine everlasting light, and the days of thy mourning shall be ended.", insight:"No more sunset, no more sorrow. The two great griefs of life are both finished."},
{q:"What shall the little one and the small one become?", options:["A thousand, and a strong nation","A prince, and a mighty king","A city, and a walled town","A garden, and a fruitful field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 60:22",verse:"A little one shall become a thousand, and a small one a strong nation: I the LORD will hasten it in his time.", insight:"God specializes in small beginnings. 'I the LORD will hasten it in his time.'"}
]}
]);
